WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.620
In 1943, in St. Louis, Carl and Gerty Cori and their colleague Arda Green

2
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.460
crystallized glycogen phosphorylase.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:18.520
It is the muscle enzyme that releases glucose units from glycogen.

4
00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:20.860
They found it in two forms.

5
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.000
Phosphorylase a was active alone.

6
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.480
Phosphorylase b worked only when AMP was present.

7
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.440
The Cori team also had an enzyme that turned a into b.

8
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.320
They assumed it clipped off a bound AMP.

9
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.600
That was not so. It was in fact a phosphatase.

10
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.040
So what did separate a from b?

11
00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:54.733
In 1955, in Seattle, Edmond Fischer and Edwin Krebs tried to make phosphorylase a by

12
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:59.893
the Cori recipe. They kept getting b, unless they filtered the extract through

13
00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:06.133
paper. The paper was leaching calcium, and extracts left standing ran out of ATP.

14
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:13.763
Over the next few years, the conversion of b to a proved to be an enzyme

15
00:01:13.763 --> 00:01:20.243
transferring one phosphate from ATP to one serine on each chain of the enzyme.

16
00:01:20.863 --> 00:01:24.743
So a carries the phosphate, and b does not.

17
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:30.923
A phosphate, added by one enzyme and removed by another, was the switch.
